WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.154
Major medical insurance is the comprehensive plan most people mean by health insurance.

00:00:05.154 --> 00:00:10.705
It covers a broad range of hospital, surgical, physician, diagnostic, prescription and preventive services,

00:00:10.705 --> 00:00:15.860
with high or unlimited maximums, in exchange for cost sharing by the insured.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.618
Historically it was sold as a supplement over a basic hospital plan

00:00:20.618 --> 00:00:25.376
or as a comprehensive plan standing alone; today the comprehensive form dominates.

00:00:25.376 --> 00:00:29.341
Federal law forbids lifetime and annual dollar limits on essential

00:00:29.341 --> 00:00:32.909
health benefits and caps the insured’s annual out-of-pocket spending.

00:00:32.909 --> 00:00:38.460
Common exclusions remain: cosmetic surgery, experimental treatment, custodial care, and adult dental and vision.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.209
A deductible is the amount the insured pays before the plan pays.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:46.375
Most are calendar-year deductibles that reset each January.

00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:50.728
A per-cause deductible applies to each separate illness or injury instead.

00:00:50.728 --> 00:00:55.873
Family plans use an individual deductible for each person and a family deductible,

00:00:55.873 --> 00:01:01.018
often two or three times the individual amount, after which everyone is covered.

00:01:01.018 --> 00:01:04.580
A carryover provision lets expenses in the last three

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.142
months of a year count toward next year’s deductible.

00:01:08.142 --> 00:01:11.704
A common accident provision applies one deductible when several

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:14.870
family members are hurt in the same accident.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.640
Preventive services are covered before the deductible.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.541
After the deductible, the plan and the insured share covered charges by percentage.

00:01:22.541 --> 00:01:25.557
In an 80/20 plan the insurer pays 80

00:01:25.557 --> 00:01:28.196
percent and the insured pays 20 percent.

00:01:28.196 --> 00:01:31.966
Coinsurance is applied to the allowed amount, not to whatever

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:35.359
the provider bills, and out-of-network coinsurance is usually higher.

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:39.507
Copayments differ from coinsurance: a copayment is a flat dollar amount

00:01:39.507 --> 00:01:43.277
per visit or prescription, often not subject to the deductible.

00:01:43.277 --> 00:01:47.047
Coinsurance keeps the insured interested in the cost of care;

00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:50.817
it also means a large claim can still leave a

00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:54.210
large bill, which is why the next provision exists.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.462
Think of the out-of-pocket maximum as a ceiling

00:01:57.462 --> 00:02:00.307
on what you pay for covered care.

00:02:00.307 --> 00:02:04.372
After you reach it, the plan pays 100 percent of

00:02:04.372 --> 00:02:08.436
covered in-network costs for the rest of the plan year.

00:02:08.436 --> 00:02:12.095
On a Marketplace plan, your deductible, coinsurance and copayments

00:02:12.095 --> 00:02:15.346
for covered in-network services count toward that limit.

00:02:15.346 --> 00:02:18.192
Your premiums and balance bills do not.

00:02:18.192 --> 00:02:22.256
Older plans also carried lifetime maximum benefits, such as one

00:02:22.256 --> 00:02:26.321
million dollars; those are now prohibited for essential health benefits.

00:02:26.321 --> 00:02:31.199
Limited policies that qualify for a federal exception may still cap benefits,

00:02:31.199 --> 00:02:35.670
which is another reason they are not substitutes for major medical.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:39.951
Marcus’s plan has a 3,000 dollar deductible, 80/20

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:43.698
coinsurance and a 6,000 dollar out-of-pocket maximum.

00:02:43.698 --> 00:02:48.514
A hospital stay produces 20,000 dollars of allowed charges.

00:02:48.514 --> 00:02:51.190
He pays the first 3,000.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:55.471
Of the remaining 17,000 he owes 20 percent,

00:02:55.471 --> 00:02:59.753
which is 3,400, for a total of 6,400.

00:02:59.753 --> 00:03:04.034
But his out-of-pocket maximum is 6,000, so he

00:03:04.034 --> 00:03:07.780
pays 6,000 and the insurer pays 14,000.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:13.132
If a second claim arrives in December, the plan pays

00:03:13.132 --> 00:03:17.948
100 percent because he has already reached the maximum.

00:03:17.948 --> 00:03:23.300
The arithmetic is always deductible first, coinsurance second, cap last.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:27.905
When major medical is layered over a basic hospital plan, a

00:03:27.905 --> 00:03:32.510
corridor deductible sits between them: the basic plan pays first, the

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:37.114
insured pays the corridor amount, then major medical pays with coinsurance.

00:03:37.114 --> 00:03:40.463
An integrated deductible instead counts what the basic

00:03:40.463 --> 00:03:43.394
plan paid toward the major medical deductible.

00:03:43.394 --> 00:03:46.743
A comprehensive major medical plan has no basic

00:03:46.743 --> 00:03:50.091
layer; one deductible and coinsurance structure covers everything.

00:03:50.091 --> 00:03:54.278
Understand both because exam questions still describe layered plans, while

00:03:54.278 --> 00:03:58.464
the policies sold today are nearly all comprehensive and must

00:03:58.464 --> 00:04:02.650
meet the federal rules on essential benefits and out-of-pocket limits.
